'''Feyn Vann''' was a [[human]] [[Gender|male]] [[scientist]] who worked for the [[Nordoxicon Unlimited]] company. Around [[19 BBY]], he was forced into [[Galactic Empire|Imperial]] service. During that [[time]], Vann worked in the [[kyber crystal research team]], a division of the [[Tarkin Initiative]] based on the [[Outer Rim Territories|Outer Rim]] [[planet]] [[Eadu]]. Guided by the expertise of [[crystallographer]] [[Galen Walton Erso|Galen Erso]], he and his colleagues [[Project Stardust|worked]] to turn [[kyber crystal]]s into a viable energy source for the [[DS-1 Orbital Battle Station|Death Star's]] [[Mk I Superlaser|superlaser]], giving it the capability to destroy entire planets. Vann's duties on Eadu included creating a set of deflectors that would drive the [[hypermatter]] flow of the station's [[Hypermatter reactor|annihilation reactor]] toward the Death Star's kyber [[crystal]]s.

In [[1 BBY]], Imperial [[Director]] [[Orson Callan Krennic|Orson Krennic]] found evidence that a member of Vann's research team had [[Treason|conspired]] with a [[Bodhi Rook|cargo pilot]] to send a message to the [[Alliance to Restore the Republic|Rebel Alliance]] about the [[Death Star]] project. The director instructed his [[Krennic's guard squadron|death troopers]] to confront the scientists, demanding that the traitor expose himself. Despite Erso admitting to the betrayal, Krennic had his [[trooper]]s [[Death|execute]] the research team, including Vann.

==Biography==
===A talented researcher===
{{Quote|It's Director Krennic. An&hellip; an unplanned visit. He's never&hellip; His shuttle will be landing any minute now.|Vann, informing Galen Erso of Director Krennic's imminent arrival|<ref name="RO 3">[[Rogue One Adaptation 3|''Rogue One Adaptation'' 3]]</ref>}}
[[File:TarkinInitiative.svg|thumb|left|215px|Feyn Vann's Imperial service was spent in a division of the Tarkin Initiative (emblem depicted).]]
The [[human]] [[Gender|male]]<ref name="RO" /> Feyn Vann was a [[scientist]] who [[Homeworld|hailed]] from [[Tri-Barr Station]]. He<ref name="Encyclopedia" /> worked as a lead researcher for the company [[Nordoxicon Unlimited]]<ref name="Geektionary" /> during the [[Republic Era|reign]] of the [[Galactic Republic]]. Following the [[Proclamation of the New Order|formation]] of the [[Galactic Empire]] in [[19 BBY]],<ref name="Date" /> he was forcibly conscripted into Imperial service to serve on the [[kyber crystal research team]], a division of the [[Tarkin Initiative]] managed by [[Director]] [[Orson Callan Krennic|Orson Krennic]]. Afterward, he became an expert in the [[kyber crystal]] research field.<ref name="ROUVG" /> Identification records for Krennic's scientists,<ref name="Dossier">''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: Rebel Dossier]]''</ref> including Vann,<ref name="ROUVG" /> were deleted from public databases upon their admittance to Imperial service.<ref name="Dossier" />

While hidden by the [[mountain]]ous terrain<ref name="SW AB">''[[Star Wars: Absolutely Everything You Need to Know, Updated and Expanded]]''</ref> of the [[Outer Rim Territories|Outer Rim]] [[planet]] [[Eadu]], Vann and his colleagues worked in the [[Eadu Energy Conversion Laboratory|Energy Conversion Laboratory]] while being kept secluded from the wider [[The galaxy|galaxy]].<ref name="ROUVG" /> The kyber crystal research team, including Vann, labored on a [[Project Stardust|project]] that would utilize kyber [[crystal]]s as an energy source for the [[Mk I Superlaser|superlaser]] of the [[DS-1 Orbital Battle Station|Death Star]], a [[Geonosian]]-designed [[battle station]] that would, with the [[superlaser]], possess the capability to destroy entire planets. While working in Eadu's [[Laboratory|laboratories]], Vann worked to create a beam at the end of the [[Death Star]]'s energy bombardment of kyber crystals.<ref name="Geektionary" />

The kyber crystal research team's [[Standard day|day]]-to-day needs on Eadu were managed by its operations manager [[Sirro Argonne]].<ref name="ROUVG" /> By [[13 BBY]],<ref name="CaptureDate">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'' dates the [[Capture of Galen Erso|capture]] of [[Galen Walton Erso|Galen Erso]] to [[13 BBY]].</ref> work on the superlaser and Vann's<ref name="Geektionary" /> [[Hypermatter reactor|annihilation reactor]] had stalled<ref name="Catalyst">''[[Catalyst: A Rogue One Novel]]''</ref> and left the project [[Standard year|years]] behind schedule.<ref name="Rise">''[[Star Wars: The Rise and Fall of the Galactic Empire]]''</ref> As a result, Krennic [[Capture of Galen Erso|hunted down]] his former colleague, the [[crystallographer]] [[Galen Walton Erso|Galen Erso]], and forced him back into Imperial service.<ref name="Geektionary" /> Vann and his team proved able to complete the Death Star's superlaser after several years working under Erso, aided by the [[Crystallography|crystallographer's]] extraordinary intellect.<ref name="RO NOVEL">[[Rogue One: A Star Wars Story (novelization)|''Rogue One: A Star Wars Story'' novelization]]</ref> Vann's expertise in shield technology allowed him to create a series of deflector arrays that redirected [[hypermatter]] streams from the superweapon's core reactor up to a crystalline firing array.<ref name="ROUVG" />

===Firing squad===
{{Quote|Gentlemen, one of you betrayed the Empire. One of you has conspired with a pilot to send messages to the Rebellion. And I urge that traitor to step forward.|Director Orson Krennic, confronting Feyn Vann and his colleagues|audio=KrennicScientistsEadu.flac|<ref name="RO" />}}
[[File:GalenErsoAndFellowScientists-RogueOne3.png|thumb|right|180px|Feyn Vann (right) and his team find out about Orson Krennic's unplanned visit.]]
In [[1 BBY]],<ref name="Date" /> [[Grand Moff]] [[Wilhuff Tarkin]] informed Krennic that a [[Alliance to Restore the Republic|rebel]] [[Treason|sympathizer]] within Erso's research team had conspired with a [[Bodhi Rook|cargo pilot]] to deliver a message to the Rebel Alliance concerning the Death Star. An enraged Krennic traveled to Eadu with his [[Krennic's guard squadron|squadron]] of [[death trooper]]s to investigate,<ref name="RO" /> informing the laboratory's workers, including Vann, of his [[ST-149|shuttle's]] impending arrival. Krennic's visit deeply worried Vann, knowing that Krennic never came to their laboratory for unplanned visits.<ref name="RO 3">[[Rogue One Adaptation 3|''Rogue One Adaptation'' 3]]</ref> [[Stormtrooper]]s ushered the scientists out of the laboratories<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> to near the entrance of Krennic's [[Delta-class T-3c shuttle|shuttle]].<ref name="RO JUNIOR">''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: A Junior Novel]]''</ref> The director then thought of each scientist individually before ordering his death [[trooper]] unit to assemble near them.<ref name="RO NOVEL" />

Krennic announced his knowledge of the conspiracy against the Empire and demanded that the traitor expose himself.<ref name="RO" /> As the director surveyed Erso's team for signs of guilt, he noticed none, including Vann, showed remorse.<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> After pausing and receiving no response, Krennic concluded that the scientists had collaboratively turned traitor. As Krennic's death troopers readied themselves to [[Death|execute]] the scientists, Erso intervened and confessed to being the traitor in an attempt to save Vann and his colleagues. However, as soon as Erso stepped toward the director, his scientists were shot and [[Death penalty|killed]] by the death troopers per Krennic's orders.<ref name="RO" />

Vann's hypermatter reactors and deflectors<ref name="Geektionary" /> contributed to the completion of the Death Star project<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> and were used by the [[space station]] during its subsequent deployments. However, the Rebel Alliance ultimately [[Battle of Yavin|destroyed]] the Death Star not long after the scientist's death by targeting a faulty [[thermal exhaust port]]<ref name="Geektionary" /> that Erso had deliberately planted inside.<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> In [[35 ABY]],<ref name="RiseDate">{{RiseAndFallDate}}</ref> the [[historian]] [[Beaumont Kin]] negatively compared Vann's team to Erso in<ref name="Rise" /> his<ref name="Encyclopedia" /> [[book]] ''[[The Rise and Fall of the Galactic Empire]]''.<ref name="Rise" />

==Personality and traits==
{{Quote|In Erso's absence Krennic was forced to try and persevere with his other captive scientists, none of whom possessed the raw intellect of the man he had lost.|Beaumont Kin, ''The Rise and Fall of the Galactic Empire''|<ref name="Rise" />}}
Feyn Vann had [[hair]] that had turned [[Color|gray]], brown [[eye]]s, and light skin.<ref name="ROUVG" /> The time he spent in the kyber crystal research team's laboratories on Eadu left him frail and unused to the outdoors by the time of his death.<ref name="RO JUNIOR" /> In his book, Kin expressed his opinion that neither Vann nor any of Krennic's other captive scientists possessed Erso's "raw intellect."<ref name="Rise" /> 

==Skills and abilities==
Vann was a talented scientific specialist who worked as one of Nordoxicon Unlimited's lead researchers. While working in the kyber crystal research field, he became exceptionally skilled at working with hypermatter reactors and deflectors.<ref name="Geektionary" />

==Equipment==
[[File:EaduScientists.png|thumb|left|250px|Vann (right) wore a blue-and-white engineering jumpsuit while part of the kyber crystal research team.]]
While on Eadu, Vann donned a long-sleeved, blue-and-white engineering [[jumpsuit]].<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> The scientist's [[Clothing|attire]] featured [[antiseptic coveralls]] that sported the dark-gray [[Imperial crest|insignia]] of the Empire on the left sleeve and a chest pocket that held two [[code cylinder]]s programmed with access to restricted areas.<ref name="ROUVG" /> The fractured kyber crystal&ndash;inspired<ref name="SW AB" /> logo of the Tarkin Initiative was present on the chest of Vann's coveralls. The scientist's uniform included shoes that were grounded against potential static charges present within laboratories. He also donned a tunic that would change colors when in the presence of harmful [[radiation]].<ref name="ROUVG" />

==Behind the scenes==
{{Quote|For the Visual Guide, gave a bunch of these scientists names that were basically phonemes made up of Los Alamos scientists' names chopped up, tossed into a blender and stuck together again.|Pablo Hidalgo|<ref name="Twitter">{{Twitter|username=pabl0hidalgo|url=status/1727942382644256970|name=[[Pablo Hidalgo]]|date=2023-11-24|quote=For the Visual Guide, gave a bunch of these scientists names that were basically phonemes made up of Los Alamos scientists' names chopped up, tossed into a blender and stuck together again.|archivefile=File:PabloLosAlamosTweet.png}}</ref>}}
[[File:EaduScientistsConcept.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Jon McCoy's concept artwork of the Eadu scientists' deaths for ''Rogue One: A Star Wars Story''.]]
Feyn Vann was first indirectly mentioned in ''[[Catalyst: A Rogue One Novel]]'', a prequel novel to the [[Star Wars Anthology Series|''Star Wars'' Anthology]] film ''[[Rogue One: A Star Wars Story]]'',<ref name="Catalyst" /> that was released on [[November 15]], [[2016]], and written by [[James Luceno]]. On [[December 16]], 2016,<ref name="RO release dates">{{SW|url=news/rogue-one-and-star-wars-40th-publishing-programs-revealed|text=NYCC 2016: ''Rogue One'' and ''Star Wars'' 40th Anniversary Publishing Programs Revealed}}</ref> he made his first full appearance in ''Rogue One: A Star Wars Story'',<ref name="RO" /> as well as in its [[Rogue One: A Star Wars Story (novelization)|adult]]<ref name="RO NOVEL" /> and [[Star Wars: Rogue One: A Junior Novel|junior novelizations]],<ref name="RO JUNIOR" /> which released the same day.<ref name="RO release dates" /> He was first pictured in a trailer for the film released on the official ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[Wikipedia:YouTube|YouTube]] channel on [[October 13]] of the same year.<ref name="Trailer">{{SWYouTube|sC9abcLLQpI|Rogue One: A Star Wars Story Trailer #2 (Official)|sw_url=video/rogue-one-a-star-wars-story-trailer-2}}</ref> For ''Rogue One'', concept artwork of the scientists' death scene was created by artist [[Jon McCoy]].<ref name="Artbook">''[[The Art of Rogue One: A Star Wars Story]]''</ref>

Vann was portrayed by [[Paul Weston]], an actor who had previously portrayed the [[Skiff guard|Skiff Guard]] [[Vedain]] in<ref name="Weston">{{WebCite|url=http://www.jedinews.co.uk/conventions-events/articles/paul-weston-sign-star-wars-fan-fun-day|text=Paul Weston To Sign At 'Star Wars' Fan Fun Day|author=[https://www.jedinews.com/author/brian-cameron/ Brian Cameron]|date=2017-01-19|work=[http://www.jedinews.co.uk Jedi News]|archivedate=20170127063017|nolive=1}}</ref> the [[1983]] film<ref name="Film VI">{{Film|VI}}</ref> [[Star Wars: Episode VI Return of the Jedi|''Star Wars'': Episode VI ''Return of the Jedi'']].<ref name="Weston" /> Vann's name was revealed in both ''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: The Ultimate Visual Guide]]'', an accompanying reference book written by [[Pablo Hidalgo]],<ref name="ROUVG" /> and in ''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: Secret Mission]]'', a [[Dorling Kindersley|DK Readers]] title authored by [[Jason Fry]],<ref name="ROSM">''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: Secret Mission]]''</ref> which were both released alongside the film on December 16, 2016.<ref name="SW.com">{{SW|url=news/rebel-reading-rogue-one-books|text=Rebel Reading: A Guide to ''Rogue One'' Books|author=[[James Floyd|Floyd, James]]|date=2016-12-14}}</ref> In [[Marvel Comics]]' [[Rogue One Adaptation 3|third issue]] of the six-part [[Star Wars comic books|comic book]] miniseries [[Star Wars: Rogue One Adaptation|adaptation]] of ''Rogue One'', Vann was mistakenly portrayed as having blue eyes. The issue was written by [[Jody Houser]], illustrated by [[Emilio Laiso]],<ref name="RO 3" /> and released on [[April 5]], [[2017]].<ref name="Marvel1">{{Marvel|url=comics/issue/62971/star_wars_rogue_one_adaptation_2017_1|text=Star Wars: Rogue One Adaptation (2017) #1}}</ref> Hidalgo created several of the scientists' names by combining elements from the names of the real-life scientists associated with the [[Wikipedia:Los Alamos National Laboratory|Los Alamos National Laboratory]].<ref name="Twitter" />

